$g(x)=2f(3x-1)=2f\big(3(x-\tfrac13)\big)$, so $g$ is obtained from $f$ by:
- a horizontal dilation by a factor of $\tfrac13$ (from the $y$-axis): $P(-1,-2)\to(-\tfrac13,-2)$;
- a translation $\tfrac13$ unit to the right: $(-\tfrac13,-2)\to(0,-2)$;
- a vertical dilation by a factor of $2$ (from the $x$-axis): $(0,-2)\to(0,-4)$.

So $P'=(0,-4)$. (Check: $g(0)=2f(-1)=2(-2)=-4$.)

Features:
- vertical asymptote $x=1$ (and $y\to+\infty$ on both sides of it);
- horizontal asymptote $y=1$ (from part (a); the curve approaches it from below as $x\to-\infty$ and from above as $x\to+\infty$);
- local minimum at the origin $(0,0)$, which is also the only intercept;
- $y\ge0$ everywhere; decreasing for $x<0$, increasing for $0<x<1$ and decreasing for $x>1$ (see graph).
